// Package goncini runs apps built with goncini's components: it loads their
// environment and config, gives them a logger, a router, a kernel and a
// server, and runs their commands. docs/layout.md in the repository
// describes the layout of an app.
//
// An app's main function is one line:
//
//	func main() { goncini.Main(config.Load, app.Build) }
//
// # Environment and config
//
// An app's config is code: a Config struct, and a Load function that fills
// it for an Env, with what every environment shares, then what the
// environment named by APP_ENV changes. Environment variables only carry
// APP_ENV and secrets, which Load reads with Env.Secret, from the process or
// from .env files. A missing secret or an unknown environment fails at boot,
// with every missing secret at once. See LoadEnv.
//
// # Wiring
//
// An app's Build is an effect-go layer injector:
//
//	panic(layer.Build(goncini.Framework, Services, Autoconfigured))
//
// Framework is goncini's providers. The app provides its own services, the
// services of each kind goncini uses ([]routing.Routes,
// []httpkernel.ErrorMapper, []console.Command, []Check), and the config sections
// that goncini's providers take (HTTP and Log), whose zero values are
// sensible defaults. It replaces any of goncini's providers by passing its
// own to layer.Build: an httpkernel.Renderer for an error format of its
// own, []httpkernel.Middleware, an httpkernel.Validator.
//
// # Commands
//
// The app's binary is its console. Run lists the commands goncini adds to
// the app's own: serve, the default, debug:router, debug:config, list and
// help. A mistake in the command line is printed, and Main exits with status
// 2.
package goncini
